<div class="hero-lead"><p class="hero-kicker">Business and founder disputes</p><h1>When business interests diverge, ordinary access can become commercial leverage.</h1><p>A personal email may recover a business account. A phone may authenticate both private and work services. A folder shared during a partnership may still reveal confidential documents or plans you intend to keep private after the relationship changes.</p><p><a class="btn" href="#contact">Request a private conversation</a></p><p class="trust">In person &#xB7; With you present &#xB7; You control your credentials</p><p>You may already have difficult decisions to make about the business. Dr. Lior Tabansky works with you, in person, on the personal digital access connected to those decisions. He explains what matters and helps you work through the changes you are authorized to make.</p></div></div></section><nav class="page-index wrap" aria-label="On this page" data-presentation-ui><a href="#see-where-personal-and-business-access-still-meet">Your situation</a><a href="#put-the-technical-questions-in-experienced-hands">First paid visit</a><a href="#contact">Contact</a></nav>
<section class="copy-section" id="see-where-personal-and-business-access-still-meet" aria-labelledby="see-where-personal-and-business-access-still-meet-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="see-where-personal-and-business-access-still-meet-heading">See where personal and business access still meet</h2></div><div class="section-detail">
<p>A personal identity may control business access while a work service exposes private information. Looking at either account alone can miss the relationship between them.</p>
<p>Dr. Tabansky examines the relevant connections across your devices, identities and services, including what they permit and what depends on them. The work gives you a basis for separating access deliberately while accounting for services you still need. It stays within your lawful authority and agreed scope.</p>
<p>A connection does not establish that a partner, competitor or adviser has used access improperly.</p>
</div></div></section>
<section class="copy-section" id="understand-what-a-change-will-affect-before-you-approve-it" aria-labelledby="understand-what-a-change-will-affect-before-you-approve-it-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="understand-what-a-change-will-affect-before-you-approve-it-heading">Understand what a change will affect before you approve it</h2></div><div class="section-detail">
<p>A password change can leave a recovery address or shared session unchanged. Removing an account can interrupt services you still use.</p>
<p>We trace the relevant identities, permissions and recovery routes, explain what they allow, and agree the sequence with you. You gain a clearer basis for changing access without having to work out those dependencies alone. Work beyond the agreed scope or price requires fresh agreement.</p>
</div></div></section>
